# {{PRESENTATION_NAME}}

A {{PRESENTATION_NAME}} turns user intent into use-case calls, and use-case
results and state into something a component can render. **UI logic only.**
See `hexagonal-architecture` (driving adapters are thin) and
`separation-of-concerns` (which side a piece of logic goes on).

## What belongs here — and what does not

| Belongs in the {{PRESENTATION_NAME}} | Does not → where it goes |
|---|---|
| Calling a use case in response to a user action | A rule about an entity ("only editable once analysed", "deleting the last item deletes the parent") → the domain |
| Form setup, defaults, submit, reset | Any state setter call → a use case that performs the mutation |
| Mapping an error **kind** to a message | Comparing an error's message text → match its kind |
| Local, transient UI flags (dialog open, a spinner for a one-off action) | Operation status other screens share → written to state by the use case |
| Deriving display data (grouping, sorting for display, formatting) | Business constants and the counts they gate → a domain function the use case exposes |
| Timing wiring (debounce, auto-save trigger) that calls a use case | The policy behind it (queueing, skip no-op, "blocked unless X") → a use case, with a scheduler port |
| Navigation — the router and link API — which is UI plumbing | Navigation inside a use case → the use case returns an outcome; the {{PRESENTATION_NAME}} navigates on it |
| Calling a port the UI legitimately owns (analytics) | Importing an adapter, a repository, an HTTP client, a platform global |

About to write an `if` that decides what is *allowed*? That is a business rule.
Put it in the domain, have the use case call it, and read the answer here.

## Access

{{PRESENTATION_ACCESS}}

- Read state to render. **Never** call a state setter — every mutation is a use
  case, even a "trivial" local edit, or the rule it implies has no test and no
  owner.
- If what you need is not offered, the use case is missing a method. Report
  `STATUS: blocked` naming it; do not reach around it.

## Navigation is UI plumbing

The router and link API may be used in a {{PRESENTATION_NAME}}, never in a use
case or the domain. A use case that needs "go to X afterwards" returns the
outcome; the {{PRESENTATION_NAME}} decides the route. Route files stay thin:
they render a screen and hold no logic.

## Platform rules

{{PRESENTATION_PLATFORM_RULES}}

## Errors

Handle every result explicitly, both branches. Map the failure **by kind** to a
user-facing message (`{{ERRORS_SKILL}}`, `{{I18N_SKILL}}`). Never show raw
error text, never branch on it, never import an infrastructure error type.

## Forms

{{FORM_BINDING_RULE}}

The form's input type is **the use case's input type**, imported from where the
use case declares it — never redeclared locally, and never a response shape.

## Components hold no logic

If a component needs state, an effect or application access, it gets a
{{PRESENTATION_NAME}} (`{{COMPONENT_SKILL}}`). That includes the initial load.

## Testing

Every {{PRESENTATION_NAME}} has a test beside it (`{{TESTING_SKILL}}` → "Testing
driving adapters"). Real use cases over fake ports; observe calls with
{{SPY_TECHNIQUE}}. Assert *when and with what* use cases are called, when they
are not, and what is exposed for each outcome. Never re-assert the use case's
effects.

## Size

Around 150 lines is the signal to decompose: extract one private helper per
concern with `extract-til-you-drop`. First check whether the bulk is a business
rule that belongs in the domain. It usually is.

## Checklist before reporting

- [ ] No rule, no state setter, no adapter import, no platform global.
- [ ] Every result handled; errors mapped by kind.
- [ ] Form bound to the use case's own input type.
- [ ] Test beside it, red then green, asserting calls and exposure only.
